Even if I had not attributed you in this post, for the reader who
can't remember and who cares, it's easy to glance up-thread in most
newsreaders (and even Google).  Similarly for posting without
quotation.  I've addressed top-posting extensively already.  I agree
that seasoned and mature posters will include this information and
post in easier-to-read styles, but the hue and cry about posters who
do not is not called for.

I think there's a dangerous-world mentality at work among the people
who worry about these issues.  The order and stability of Usenet is
constantly under threat; rigorous precautions must be stridently
observed.  I don't buy it.  I've seen some groups go under because
they were easy targets obesessively worried about the problem to begin
with and thus almost asking for it (Objectivists) or because the
subject matter of the group was ill-defined and/or prone to
lawlessness to begin with (certain warez-related groups).  And note
these are cases of intentional damage.  But I've never seen a group
wrecked that has a consistent, clear subject matter with a critical
mass of rational, upfront, regular posters, whose defect is that some
posters are less-versed in the patterns of Usenet and thus post
idiosyncratically or casually.  The "wreckage" simply doesn't happen
unless a.) the mass of regular posters makes it into an issue, and b.)
the mass of casual posters is *very* casual and reaches a
supermajority.  a.) occurs on r.g.r.n, but b.) isn't close to
happening, and wouldn't be even without the netcops here.  And it's
entirely within the power of the regular posters to put a stop to a.).

The "problem" is much more related to the mentality of the
historically established posters than to any immutable principle of
human group behavior or Usenet evil.

